{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791382593,"nonce":"yaQS/k0q10Q47lR4Ls0lRQUeuFc=","message":"Landed as 76bc7ca9bb0884e41b4b835e0cfe65dc3bfb707d, based on 9e0fa0e7b8c172ce5301b6cd7f3f9ac847a4a649.\n\nThe accepted version prices private constant-specialized bodies only for otherwise oversized callees at loop call sites. It requires a bound integer arithmetic operand before copying the callee and after constant folding/CFG pruning, so opcode-only dispatch does not qualify. Existing inlining budgets still apply. Same-type integer/bool constants substitute both signature ids and distinct Parameter(i) SSA aliases; original callees remain available for dynamic callers. Cache/source-body bounds prevent unbounded analysis/code growth. ZYNTAX_DISABLE_CONSTANT_INLINE provides a safe diagnostic control.\n\nRejected intermediate variants: loop sites alone improved FFT but regressed sparse by about 7%; requiring a single-block residual lost most FFT gains and still cost sparse; requiring an arithmetic operand only after cloning left unnecessary analysis work. The final early filter removes that analysis for opcode-only sites. No frontend, representation, ABI, promotion threshold, or floating-point policy changes.\n\nNUC i5-1250P, CPUs 0-7, serialized with /tmp/zyntax-nuc-run and /tmp/zyntax-nuc.lock. Primary comparisons explicitly unset ZYPY_LLVM and ZYLUA_LLVM, and backend checks report cranelift. Final five alternating process repetitions per arm; before = saved /tmp/zypy-load-ranges, off = candidate with specialization disabled, after = candidate default. Times are median milliseconds. Short means one SciMark trial/process; longer means five trials/process, including the initial interpreted/tier-up work.\n\nFFT short kernel before/off/after: 344.387 / 344.801 / 301.295; wall 534.821 / 535.645 / 492.847.\nFFT longer mean kernel: 287.158 / 284.138 / 239.167; whole-process wall 1630.844 / 1610.193 / 1387.922. Versus the committed baseline this is 7.8% less short wall time and 14.9% less longer wall time; same-executable controls confirm the transformation's effect.\nSparse short kernel: 145.362 / 146.428 / 139.643; wall 335.715 / 337.658 / 328.191. Longer mean: 100.678 / 100.210 / 100.883; wall 692.957 / 689.043 / 692.919. Do not claim a repeatable sparse gain: the earlier five-repetition screen was effectively unchanged in the longer run.\n\nFull Cranelift-only screen: 17 Lua checks and all 22 supported Python checks match reference answers. pidigits remains unsupported. Lua performance is mostly unchanged; op_overload timing is bimodal and is not counted as a stable win. The full Python fast screen and focused repeats exposed about 1-4% between-build costs in crypto_pyaes and pyflate-fast. Three-arm longer controls reduced those gaps to crypto wall 837.685 / 829.740 / 840.563 and pyflate 15607.889 / 15731.142 / 15662.298. These small differences remain measurement/build uncertainty, not proof of zero regression. Runtime on/off does not show a consistent penalty. Rebuilding the snapshot twice with the current identical builder and the flag off/on produces byte-identical optimization traces (8032 inline decisions); no snapshot inline site changes. Snapshot bytes vary between invocations and were not treated as a semantic comparison.\n\nFannkuch appeared about 5% faster but all 77 emitted native functions have identical instruction streams after normalizing wide address/OSR-site immediates. Do not credit that as a specialization gain; the existing between-build investigation remains open.\n\nLLVM-enabled regression screen also improves FFT: short wall 488.939 -\u003e 452.870 and longer wall 1184.206 -\u003e 1028.093; sparse longer wall 564.525 -\u003e 559.894. TieredRuntime still starts in the interpreter.\n\nValidation: on both macOS ARM64 and NUC x86, all 19 inliner tests and Lua 43/Python 50 conformance categories pass with LLVM off and on. The three official Lua suites were explicitly excluded. NUC additionally passes 3 loop-specialization tests and 14 OSR transfer tests. New regression exercises repeated constants, a distinct parameter SSA id, dynamic fallback callers, checked residual arms, a cold caller, original-callee preservation, and execution through interpreter/Cranelift/LLVM. rustfmt (2024) and git diff --check pass.\n\nRaw artifacts on NUC and copied locally: /tmp/zyntax-nuc-constant-loop-controls-final-python-{all,standard}.json, /tmp/zyntax-nuc-constant-loop-v4-{lua,python-all,python-standard}.json, /tmp/zyntax-nuc-constant-loop-final-python-all.json, /tmp/zyntax-nuc-constant-loop-controls-python-{all,standard}.json, /tmp/zyntax-nuc-constant-loop-llvm-python-{all,standard}.json. Snapshot traces: /tmp/zyntax-snapshot-constant-{off,on}.trace. Source SHA256 for inline.rs is dd0486394b43986d518bbc836ed8c3610558b89d1de63dbafcc49cd7904d8f03 on both machines.\n\nThis closes the bounded constant-call profitability change. It does not close the PyPy/LuaJIT performance gap: FFT remains slower than PyPy; dynamic fields, boxing, and short-run overhead remain separate tracked work.","files":null}]}